WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.100
DynamoDB splits a payments table into partitions.

2
00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:11.375
Each one holds a range of key hashes, and each has its own budget of writes.

3
00:00:11.567 --> 00:00:17.675
Provision 400 writes a second, and the four partitions each get an equal share, 100.

4
00:00:17.867 --> 00:00:19.896
But traffic is rarely even.

5
00:00:19.896 --> 00:00:25.005
Partition four gets 150 writes a second, and the others get 50 each.

6
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:34.785
Partition four can't pass its share, so DynamoDB refuses fifty writes a second, while the table uses only 300 of its 400.

7
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.918
Adaptive capacity fixes this case.

8
00:00:37.918 --> 00:00:44.528
The table has unused units, so DynamoDB raises partition four's share to 150.

9
00:00:44.733 --> 00:00:53.679
If a partition stays busy, DynamoDB also splits it, at a key chosen from its traffic, so the busy keys land on both halves.

10
00:00:53.867 --> 00:01:02.215
Now a single item gets hot: the running total for merchant m-17, written 1,500 times a second.

11
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:06.407
Every partition has a hard ceiling of 1,000 writes a second.

12
00:01:06.407 --> 00:01:08.877
Borrowing from the table stops there.

13
00:01:09.067 --> 00:01:13.506
A split cuts a range of keys in two, but one key can't be cut.

14
00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:19.235
DynamoDB can move the item into a partition of its own, and it still gets 1,000.

15
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:24.783
So 500 writes a second are refused, however much capacity the table has.

16
00:01:24.967 --> 00:01:27.028
Every partition has a ceiling.

17
00:01:27.028 --> 00:01:33.556
DynamoDB moves capacity and splits ranges for you, but one hot key you have to spread yourself.
